{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's distinction between 'informational content' and 'predictive power' collapses under the requirement to 'act on' a theory. Popper explicitly links high empirical content to low logical probability (high improbability). If a decision-maker prefers Theory A over Theory B because A has survived severe tests (high corroboration/content), they are necessarily betting that A will continue to yield successful predictions in future applications. This expectation of future success based on past performance is the definition of induction. By framing this as a 'wager' on 'information density' rather than 'probability of being true,' the target attempts to semantically bypass the inductive leap required for action, but it cannot escape the fact that acting on a corroborated theory assumes the future will resemble the past regarding that theory's validity.","premise":"Acting on a theory requires an expectation of its future predictive success.","successor_question":"If the preference for the better-corroborated theory is not based on an expectation of future success (induction), what rational basis remains for choosing it over a falsified or untested theory when lives or resources are at stake?","target":"354fa70c0e0698475f3990a6611ddf1b45577f6071bdf6fcf8f9490f23ab5688"}]}